From: j7
Subject: j7: ask the gauge on the buses the SoC actually has

The last image was built with this patch in the tree but the build log says it never ran:

    [warning] Skipping patch .../0400-j7-ask-the-gauge-where-the-bus-map-says-it-is.patch
    (already upstream, previously applied, or malformed):
    error: patch failed: bsd/dev/a5xelte_battery.c

and the boot log confirms it, because the scan printed the old table again - the three S3C
controllers, `0x13860000`-`0x13890000` driven as high-speed, and no `0x10510000` at all.

The reason is the first hunk.  0400 was written from 0393's diff and asked to add
`fg_addr << 1` to the S3C path, but 0391 already put exactly that there, so the hunk did not
apply and the whole patch - the part that matters included - was dropped.  The address form
needs no change after all; the table does.

**The bus table mixes the two I2C blocks this SoC has.**  Per the Exynos 7870 device tree,
`0x13830000`-`0x13890000` are the six S3C2440 controllers (i2c0-i2c5), `0x138a0000`-`0x138c0000`
are hsi2c1-hsi2c3, `0x138d0000`/`0x138e0000` are i2c7/i2c8, `0x138f0000`/`0x13950000`/`0x13960000`
are hsi2c4-hsi2c6, and **hsi2c0 - the controller the PMIC sits on - is `0x10510000`, in the MIF
domain, and was never probed at all.**  Four S3C controllers were driven through the high-speed
register map, which is why they read back the `ctl=0x9` that was written to IICCON, and the three
real high-speed ones in the table are hsi2c1-hsi2c3, which the bootloader never clocks.

Each bus is now listed with its own register map, and hsi2c0 - the one bus whose clock and pinmux
the bootloader proved by using it to bring the phone up - is asked first.  The gauge register map
is unchanged: RSOC 0x08, RVBAT 0x04, RCUR_CC 0x06, RBATCAP 0x0e.

Co-Authored-By: dipa-studio <183933817+dipa-studio@users.noreply.github.com>

--- a/bsd/dev/a5xelte_battery.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/a5xelte_battery.c
@@ -407,10 +407,17 @@
 	 * Exynos 7870 boards.  Ask every address a fuel gauge can have on every controller,
 	 * the bus Samsung documents first, and keep the first pair that ACKs. */
 	static const struct { uint32_t pa; int hsi2c; } buses[] = {
+		/* hsi2c0, 0x10510000: the PMIC bus, the only controller the bootloader is
+		 * known to have left clocked and pinmuxed, so the gauge is asked here first. */
+		{ 0x10510000u, 1 },
+		/* hsi2c1-hsi2c6. */
+		{ 0x138a0000u, 1 }, { 0x138b0000u, 1 }, { 0x138c0000u, 1 },
+		{ 0x138f0000u, 1 }, { 0x13950000u, 1 }, { 0x13960000u, 1 },
+		/* The S3C2440 controllers: i2c0-i2c5, i2c7, i2c8.  They need their pins
+		 * muxed before a transfer moves, which is the stat=0xf0 in the last log. */
 		{ 0x13830000u, 0 }, { 0x13840000u, 0 }, { 0x13850000u, 0 },
-		{ 0x13860000u, 1 }, { 0x13870000u, 1 }, { 0x13880000u, 1 },
-		{ 0x13890000u, 1 }, { 0x138a0000u, 1 }, { 0x138b0000u, 1 },
-		{ 0x138c0000u, 1 },
+		{ 0x13860000u, 0 }, { 0x13870000u, 0 }, { 0x13880000u, 0 },
+		{ 0x13890000u, 0 }, { 0x138d0000u, 0 }, { 0x138e0000u, 0 },
 	};
 	static const uint8_t addrs[] = { 0x3bu, 0x36u, 0x33u };
 
